version: 2
title: How the hook works
description: claude/hook.mjs as what happens to it — a session starts, a prompt is submitted, a book or the wiring changes — and, under each, what the hook does, step by step, with the moments that can arise in a step written into it.
decisions: []
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    content:
      key: glance
      label: The hook at a glance
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The hook at a glance
        sections:
          - title: What it is
            body: |
              `claude/hook.mjs` is a Node ES module that Claude Code runs as a command hook at two
              moments: when a session starts, and before every prompt. Each run reads the two books
              beside it — `claude.playbook`, the master, and `memory.playbook`, the memory — and
              writes one piece of text that Claude Code puts in front of Claude: the whole map at a
              session's start, a one-paragraph reminder before each prompt. Nothing is kept between
              runs; the books are read as they are on disk at that moment.
          - title: Where it is wired
            body: |
              `.claude/settings.json` at the repository root, a file git tracks, so every session
              opened with this repository as its working directory has it:

              | hook | command | timeout | spinner |
              |---|---|---|---|
              | `SessionStart` | `node "C:/Github/studio-kinds/claude/hook.mjs" session` | 15 s | Reading the Studio's master playbook |
              | `UserPromptSubmit` | `node "C:/Github/studio-kinds/claude/hook.mjs" prompt` | 15 s | — |

              The same file sets `autoMemoryEnabled: false`: Claude Code keeps no memory of its own
              here — the books are the memory. The command names the script by its absolute path,
              so the wiring is this machine's.
          - title: What it reads
            body: |
              The two books in its own folder, `js-yaml` from the workspace's install under
              `packages/filekinds`, and one command-line argument: `prompt` for the reminder;
              `session`, anything else, or nothing for the map. Nothing from stdin: the JSON
              Claude Code pipes in — session id, working directory, how the session started, the
              prompt's text — is not read.
          - title: How a book is read
            body: |
              The same for the master and the memory, on every run. The hook lists; Claude opens
              the book to read.
            children:
              - title: The file is parsed
                body: |
                  The file as UTF-8 text through js-yaml's `load`: the whole YAML document,
                  whatever its shape. No kind engine runs, no checker, and the `version` line is
                  not read.
              - title: The events are taken
                body: |
                  The top-level `events` list; when it is not a list, the book has no events. Of
                  each event only two things are used: its `label`, and its `content`. Not read:
                  the master's own decisions, an event's `when`, `hint`, `trigger` and `domain`,
                  and every document's body.
              - title: An event's content is named
                body: |
                  A mapping is one entry (version 2); a list is each of its members (version 1);
                  anything else is none. A `file` entry is named by its file, with " by" and its
                  decisions when it varies; a written entry by its `label`, else its `key`, else
                  "content", followed by its `kind` in parentheses, `md` when none is given. In
                  the map the names follow the event's label after an arrow, separated by commas;
                  a master event with none reads "(no content yet)", a memory event with none
                  shows nothing after the arrow.
              - title: A child book's questions are gathered
                body: |
                  An entry counts when its `kind` is exactly `playbook` and its `doc` is a
                  mapping. Its `doc.decisions` are listed, each by its label and its values'
                  labels (a value's key when it has no label); a child with no decisions is left
                  out. The line is headed by the child's `title`, else the entry's label, else the
                  event's.

                  Only a child book written directly under a master event as `doc` is read: a set
                  of child books (`by` + `docs`) is passed over, a book nested inside a child is
                  not walked, and the memory's child books are not searched. Gathered on every
                  run; written out only in the map.
              - title: The memory's areas are taken
                body: |
                  The same `events` list from `memory.playbook`, named the same way. In the
                  reminder each label stands without its leading "Working on: ". Nothing else of
                  the memory is read.
          - title: What it writes
            body: |
              One JSON object on stdout, and exit code 0, every time:

              ```json
              {"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "SessionStart", "additionalContext": "<the text>"}}
              ```

              `hookEventName` is `SessionStart` or `UserPromptSubmit` by the argument. Claude Code
              takes `additionalContext` and adds it to Claude's context. A missing or unparsable
              master is reported the same way — as text under the same event name, still with
              exit 0 — never as a hook failure.
          - title: What it never does
            body: |
              Read stdin. Write a file. Block a prompt. Keep anything between runs. Throw over a
              book that is missing or will not parse. A book that parses into a shape it does not
              expect is the one thing that can still crash it — the moments under each reading
              step say which.
  - key: session-starts
    label: A session starts
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Running
    detail: From SessionStart firing to the map in front of Claude — and, in each step, what can arise.
    content:
      key: session
      label: At a session's start
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: At a session's start
        sections:
          - title: 1 · Claude Code runs the hook
            body: |
              SessionStart fires at startup, on resume, after `/clear`, after `/compact` and on a
              fork; the entry in `.claude/settings.json` has no `matcher`, so it runs for all of
              them. Claude Code runs

              ```
              node "C:/Github/studio-kinds/claude/hook.mjs" session
              ```

              with a 15-second timeout, showing "Reading the Studio's master playbook" in the
              spinner meanwhile. It pipes a JSON object to the script's stdin — session id,
              transcript path, working directory, how the session started — which the script does
              not read: the argument `session` is all it goes by. A run that outlasts its 15
              seconds is cut off and adds nothing.
          - title: 2 · The hook finds its folder and js-yaml
            body: |
              The folder is its own, read off the module's URL — the pathname without the slash
              before the drive letter. The master is `claude.playbook` in it, the memory
              `memory.playbook`. A folder whose path a URL escapes, one with a space, is not found.

              js-yaml comes from a `require` anchored at `packages/filekinds/package.json`, two
              folders up from the script, so the package is filekinds' install.
            content:
              kind: playbook
              doc:
                version: 2
                title: While js-yaml is found
                decisions: []
                events:
                  - key: no-yaml
                    label: js-yaml cannot be found
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The `require` from `packages/filekinds` fails — the workspace has no install — and
                        the run goes on with no parser: each book is read and yields nothing, and no note
                        names the cause. The map is the header, an empty line where the events would be,
                        the QUESTIONS sentence with "(no child book carries a decision)", and no MEMORY.
                        An empty master, or one whose `events` is not a list, gives the same text. The cure
                        is `pnpm install` at the repository root.
          - title: 3 · The master is read
            body: |
              `claude.playbook` in the folder, as UTF-8 text through js-yaml. Parsed, its events'
              labels, the names of their content and the child books' questions are kept — the
              rules are under Always, "How a book is read". What can arise while it is read is the
              book below.
            content:
              kind: playbook
              doc:
                version: 2
                title: While the master is read
                decisions: []
                events:
                  - key: master-missing
                    label: The master playbook is missing
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        No file at `<folder>/claude.playbook`. The whole text, under `SessionStart`, exit 0:

                        ```
                        Master playbook missing at <path> — recreate it from the template (studio-check --template playbook) before working.
                        ```

                        The memory is not read. Claude sees this line in place of the map.
                  - key: master-broken
                    label: The master playbook does not parse
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The file is there but js-yaml refuses it. The whole text, under `SessionStart`, exit 0:

                        ```
                        Master playbook at <path> did not parse: <the parser's message, with the line and a caret>. Read it raw and fix it first.
                        ```

                        The memory is not read.
                  - key: master-shape
                    label: A child book's decisions are not a list
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The YAML parses, but a child book's `decisions` — or a decision's `values` — is a
                        mapping instead of a list. The run crashes: exit 1, nothing on stdout, the error on
                        stderr. Claude Code notes a hook error in the transcript and Claude gets no map for
                        this session. `studio-check claude/claude.playbook` refuses the shape; the hook does
                        not run the checker, so a book saved past it is what the next run reads.
          - title: 4 · The memory is read
            body: |
              `memory.playbook` in the folder, the same way. Parsed, its events' labels and the
              names of their content are kept for the MEMORY section. What can arise while it is
              read is the book below.
            content:
              kind: playbook
              doc:
                version: 2
                title: While the memory is read
                decisions: []
                events:
                  - key: memory-missing
                    label: The memory playbook is missing
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        Skipped, nothing said. The map has no MEMORY section.
                  - key: memory-broken
                    label: The memory playbook does not parse
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        A note is kept and the run goes on as if the memory were absent: the MEMORY section
                        is absent, and the last line of the map is

                        ```
                        Memory playbook at <path> did not parse: <the parser's message>. Read it raw and fix it first.
                        ```
                  - key: memory-shape
                    label: A memory event has no label
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The run survives. Its line in the MEMORY section reads "undefined → …".
                        `studio-check claude/memory.playbook` refuses the shape.
          - title: 5 · The map is written
            body: |
              One text, in this order — the header, the master's events, the questions, the
              memory, the tail.
            children:
              - title: The skeleton
                body: |
                  ```
                  MASTER PLAYBOOK — <path of claude.playbook> (version 2: every checklist and child book is in the file)
                  Consult it at the start of every task and whenever one of its events happens: … studio-check after editing.
                  - <event label> → <content name>, <content name>
                  - <event label> → (no content yet)
                  QUESTIONS — the child books' decisions. None has an answer in the file …
                  - <child's title> (under "<event label>"): <question> [<answer> / <answer>]; <question> [<answer> / <answer>]
                  MEMORY — <path of memory.playbook> (version 2, one file). The brief under each event IS Claude's memory …
                  - Working on: <area> → <brief's label> (brief)
                  Memory playbook at <path> did not parse: <message>. Read it raw and fix it first.
                  ```
              - title: The header
                body: |
                  Two fixed lines: the master's path, and the standing instruction — consult it at
                  the start of every task and whenever one of its events happens, open the event's
                  content in the book and do every item; Maher adds events there in the Studio;
                  keep the checklists true; `studio-check` after editing.
              - title: The events
                body: |
                  One line per event of the master, in file order: the label, an arrow, the names
                  of its content entries separated by commas. An event with no content reads
                  "(no content yet)".
              - title: The questions
                body: |
                  A fixed sentence — none has an answer in the file, ask Maher each in chat when
                  its event fires — then one line per child book that carries decisions: the
                  child's title, "(under" the event's label ")", and each decision as its label
                  followed by its answers' labels in brackets, separated by slashes; decisions
                  separated by semicolons. When no child carries a decision the one line reads
                  "(no child book carries a decision)".
              - title: The memory
                body: |
                  Only when `memory.playbook` is there and has events: a fixed paragraph — the
                  brief under each event IS Claude's memory for that area; take an area's event
                  before working there; writes to either book happen only through "Something
                  should be committed to memory" — then one line per event of the memory, the
                  label, an arrow, the names of its content, exactly as for the master.
              - title: The tail
                body: |
                  When `memory.playbook` is there but did not parse, its note is the last line,
                  after everything else.
          - title: 6 · The map lands in Claude's context
            body: |
              The script writes one JSON object to stdout and exits 0:

              ```json
              {"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "SessionStart", "additionalContext": "<the map>"}}
              ```

              Claude Code adds `additionalContext` to Claude's context at the start of the
              session, where it reads as "SessionStart hook additional context: …". After a
              compaction the hook runs again and the map arrives again, so it survives a long
              session.
  - key: prompt-submitted
    label: A prompt is submitted
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Running
    detail: From UserPromptSubmit firing to the reminder beside the prompt — and, in each step, what can arise.
    content:
      key: prompt
      label: Before every prompt
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Before every prompt
        sections:
          - title: 1 · Claude Code runs the hook
            body: |
              UserPromptSubmit fires once per prompt, before Claude sees it. Claude Code runs

              ```
              node "C:/Github/studio-kinds/claude/hook.mjs" prompt
              ```

              with a 15-second timeout and no spinner text of its own. It pipes a JSON object to
              the script's stdin — session id, working directory, the prompt's text — which the
              script does not read: the argument `prompt` is all it goes by. A run that outlasts
              its 15 seconds is cut off, and the prompt goes through with no reminder.
          - title: 2 · The hook finds its folder and js-yaml
            body: |
              The folder is its own, read off the module's URL — the pathname without the slash
              before the drive letter. The master is `claude.playbook` in it, the memory
              `memory.playbook`. A folder whose path a URL escapes, one with a space, is not found.

              js-yaml comes from a `require` anchored at `packages/filekinds/package.json`, two
              folders up from the script, so the package is filekinds' install.
            content:
              kind: playbook
              doc:
                version: 2
                title: While js-yaml is found
                decisions: []
                events:
                  - key: no-yaml
                    label: js-yaml cannot be found
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The `require` from `packages/filekinds` fails — the workspace has no install — and
                        the run goes on with no parser: each book is read and yields nothing, and no note
                        names the cause. The reminder reads "do every item — ." with an empty list, then
                        the questions sentence. An empty master, or one whose `events` is not a list, gives
                        the same text. The cure is `pnpm install` at the repository root.
          - title: 3 · The master is read
            body: |
              `claude.playbook` in the folder, as UTF-8 text through js-yaml. Parsed, its events'
              labels are kept for the reminder — the names of their content and the child books'
              questions are gathered too, as on every run, and left out of it. The rules are under
              Always, "How a book is read". What can arise while it is read is the book below.
            content:
              kind: playbook
              doc:
                version: 2
                title: While the master is read
                decisions: []
                events:
                  - key: master-missing
                    label: The master playbook is missing
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        No file at `<folder>/claude.playbook`. The whole text, under `UserPromptSubmit`, exit 0:

                        ```
                        Master playbook missing at <path> — recreate it from the template (studio-check --template playbook) before working.
                        ```

                        The memory is not read. Claude sees this line in place of the reminder.
                  - key: master-broken
                    label: The master playbook does not parse
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The file is there but js-yaml refuses it. The whole text, under `UserPromptSubmit`, exit 0:

                        ```
                        Master playbook at <path> did not parse: <the parser's message, with the line and a caret>. Read it raw and fix it first.
                        ```

                        The memory is not read.
                  - key: master-shape
                    label: A child book's decisions are not a list
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The YAML parses, but a child book's `decisions` — or a decision's `values` — is a
                        mapping instead of a list. The run crashes: exit 1, nothing on stdout, the error on
                        stderr. Claude Code notes a hook error in the transcript, and the prompt goes through
                        with no reminder. `studio-check claude/claude.playbook` refuses the shape; the hook
                        does not run the checker, so a book saved past it is what the next run reads.
          - title: 4 · The memory is read
            body: |
              `memory.playbook` in the folder, the same way. Parsed, its events' labels are kept,
              each as an area without its leading "Working on: ". What can arise while it is read
              is the book below.
            content:
              kind: playbook
              doc:
                version: 2
                title: While the memory is read
                decisions: []
                events:
                  - key: memory-missing
                    label: The memory playbook is missing
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        Skipped, nothing said. The reminder ends its list of events with a full stop and
                        goes straight to the sentence about the questions.
                  - key: memory-broken
                    label: The memory playbook does not parse
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        Nothing is said. The reminder is the one for a missing memory — no areas, no rule
                        about writing, no note. Only the map carries the note.
                  - key: memory-shape
                    label: A memory event has no label
                    trigger: imposed
                    content:
                      kind: md
                      doc: |
                        The run crashes: exit 1, nothing on stdout, the error on stderr. Claude Code notes a
                        hook error in the transcript, and the prompt goes through with no reminder.
                        `studio-check claude/memory.playbook` refuses the shape.
          - title: 5 · The reminder is written
            body: |
              One paragraph, no line breaks, in three parts.
            children:
              - title: The skeleton
                body: |
                  ```
                  Master playbook (<path of claude.playbook>): as you work, watch for these events and, when one happens, open its content in the book and do every item — <event label> · <event label> · <event label>. MEMORY lives in <path of memory.playbook>: before touching an area, take its event (<area>, <area>, <area>) and read its document. Never write to a playbook under claude/ except through the "Something should be committed to memory" event and Maher's answer there. The child books' questions have NO standing answer (a version-2 walk is session-only, nothing is ever saved): ask Maher each question in chat and act on his answer there; the event's content follows his answer, and until he answers the event's own hint says what to do.
                  ```
              - title: Three parts
                body: |
                  1. The master's path and the standing instruction, then every event label of
                     the master joined by " · ".
                  2. With a memory that has events: the memory's path, the areas, and the rule that
                     nothing is written to either book except through "Something should be
                     committed to memory". Without one: a full stop and nothing more.
                  3. The questions rule: no standing answer, ask in chat, the content follows the
                     answer, the hint until then.
              - title: What it leaves out
                body: |
                  The questions themselves, the names of the events' content, and any note about
                  a memory that did not parse. Those are the map's alone; the reminder is the list
                  of events to watch for and where the books are.
          - title: 6 · The reminder lands in Claude's context
            body: |
              The script writes one JSON object to stdout and exits 0:

              ```json
              {"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "UserPromptSubmit", "additionalContext": "<the reminder>"}}
              ```

              Claude Code attaches `additionalContext` to the prompt, where it reads as
              "UserPromptSubmit hook additional context: …", before Claude reads the prompt. The
              prompt itself is never changed or blocked.
  - key: book-changes
    label: A book changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Changing
    content:
      kind: md
      doc: |
        Nothing is kept between runs, so the next run reads the file as it is. An event Maher
        adds in the Studio is in the reminder from his next prompt and in the map from the next
        session start — or resume, `/clear`, `/compact`. A save that leaves a book unparsable
        shows as that book's note at the next run.
  - key: wiring-changes
    label: The wiring changes
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Changing
    content:
      kind: md
      doc: |
        `.claude/settings.json` is watched by Claude Code, which picks up a hook change while
        running; the next SessionStart or UserPromptSubmit runs the command as it now reads. The
        command names the script by its absolute path: a repository moved elsewhere needs the
        path changed with it.
